Anchorage for Debian and Ubuntu
===============================

Who may do what
---------------
The package creates two empty groups. Administrators (members of "sudo") can always use
Anchorage with their own password. To let someone else use it:

  sudo adduser alice netmount      # add network shares and save logins for them
  sudo adduser alice driveadmins   # change everything (effectively root: be careful)

They need to log out and in again before the new group counts.

Beta versions
-------------
A version like 0.9.0~beta1 is a beta. It shows a warning at every start (which you can
turn off for that version), and it logs what it does. To see the log:

  anchorage                        # the app's lines appear in the terminal
  journalctl -b | grep anchorage   # the root helper's lines

Release versions log only problems; switch the details on for a bug report with
  QT_LOGGING_RULES="anchorage.*.info=true" anchorage
Passwords are never logged.

Uninstalling
------------
/etc/fstab is never changed by removing Anchorage. Everything Anchorage added to it is
plain fstab, so drives, binds and network shares keep working without Anchorage.
SMB shares that use a saved login need their file in /etc/anchorage/credentials.

  * Removing a beta asks whether to delete /etc/anchorage (saved logins, backups of
    /etc/fstab and settings). The default is Yes, but No if shares still use saved logins.
  * Removing a release keeps /etc/anchorage.
  * Purging deletes only the saved logins that /etc/fstab no longer uses, and keeps the
    backups and settings.

The groups driveadmins and netmount are left in place.
